answers.text:
Use the `never` type in the `default` branch. If every union member is handled, the value is narrowed to `never`, so assigning it to a `never` variable compiles. If you add a new member and forget a case, the value is no longer `never` and you get a compile error.

```ts
type Shape =
  | { kind: "circle"; radius: number }
  | { kind: "square"; size: number };

function area(s: Shape): number {
  switch (s.kind) {
    case "circle":
      return Math.PI * s.radius ** 2;
    case "square":
      return s.size ** 2;
    default: {
      const _exhaustive: never = s;
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}
```

Adding `{ kind: "triangle"; ... }` to `Shape` will make the `default` line fail, because `s` is now the triangle type, which can't be assigned to `never`.

A common variant is a helper:

```ts
function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error("Unexpected: " + x);
}
// default: return assertNever(s);
```

This also gives you a runtime error if an unexpected value slips through.

The TypeScript handbook describes this "exhaustiveness checking" pattern in its narrowing section. Another option is to enable `noImplicitReturns` and omit `default`, since a missing case then leaves a code path without a return. The `never` approach is more explicit and works for functions that don't return values too.

