Use a union-typed parameter. The docs say to always prefer parameters with union types instead of overloads when possible.

**Why overloads fall short:** A function with separate overloads for a string and an array can be called with either, but not with a value that might be a string or an array, because TypeScript can only resolve a call to a single overload. The docs' example fails on this call:

```ts
len(Math.random() > 0.5 ? "hello" : [0]);
```

**Preferred version:** Because both overloads have the same argument count and same return type, you can write a non-overloaded version instead.

```ts
function len(x: any[] | string) {
  return x.length;
}
```

Callers can invoke this with either sort of value, and you don't have to figure out a correct implementation signature.

The docs give a related reason: overloads cause problems for people who are "passing through" a value to your function. With separate overloads, passing a `number | string` through is incorrectly an error, while with union types it is correctly OK.

