The main difference is in how conflicts are handled. The principal difference between interface extension and intersections is how conflicts are handled, and that is typically one of the main reasons to pick an interface or a type alias of an intersection.

**Interfaces:** If properties are not compatible (the same property name but different types), TypeScript raises an error. This is shown here:

```ts
interface Person {
  name: string;
}

interface Person {
  name: number;
}
```
This code throws an error because the properties are incompatible. The merging docs say the same: the compiler issues an error if the interfaces both declare a non-function member of the same name but of different types.

**Intersection types:** Properties with different types are merged automatically. When the type is used later, TypeScript expects the property to satisfy both types simultaneously, which may produce unexpected results.

```ts
type Staff = Person1 & Person2
```
This compiles, but it results in a `never` type. Staff would require the name property to be both a string and a number, so the property is of type `never`.

**Newer behavior:** Since TypeScript 3.9, if the conflicting properties are discriminants (such as the literal `kind` fields on `Circle` and `Square`), the whole intersection type collapses to `never` rather than just the property.

