Not fully. By default, TypeScript will not check these imports for validity. If the import resolves to a valid source file, TypeScript will load and check the file. If no source file is found, TypeScript will silently ignore the import. So `import "./styles.css"` won't error by default, even if the file can't be found, which masks potential typos on side effect imports.

To catch these, enable `--noUncheckedSideEffectImports`. When enabled, TypeScript will error if it can't find a source file for a side effect import. This option is currently off by default.

With the option on, CSS imports may start to error. To work around this, you can write an ambient module declaration with a wildcard specifier in a global file:

```ts
// ./src/globals.d.ts

// Recognize all CSS files as module imports.
declare module "*.css" {}
```

