TS2341 is the error for accessing a `private` member from outside its class. The docs don't spell out the code number. The examples that carry the `@errors: 2341` annotation are all `private` access violations, and the error text reads "Property 'foo' is private and only accessible within class 'C'."

Example that triggers it:

```ts
class Base {
  private x = 0;
}
const b = new Base();
// Can't access from outside the class
console.log(b.x);
```

The same error appears in these cases:
- `private` is like `protected`, but doesn't allow access to the member even from subclasses.
- Static members can be private too, and `console.log(MyClass.x)` on a `private static x` triggers it (static members can use the same `public`, `protected`, and `private` modifiers).
- Parameter properties declared `private` give the same error when read from outside, such as `a.z` in the `Params` example.

To fix it, access the member only from inside the declaring class. You can also change its visibility modifier.

The check only happens at compile time. `private` and `protected` are only enforced during type checking. `private` also allows access using bracket notation during type checking, so `s["secretKey"]` doesn't raise the error.

