answers.text:
TS2540 is the error TypeScript raises when you assign to a `readonly` property. The docs don't spell out the error text. Their examples tagged `@errors: 2540` all show this case.

- A property marked `readonly` can't be written to during type-checking.
- For class fields, `readonly` prevents assignments to the field outside of the constructor.

Example from the docs, tagged 2540:

```ts
interface SomeType {
  readonly prop: string;
}

function doSomething(obj: SomeType) {
  // We can read from 'obj.prop'.
  console.log(`prop has the value '${obj.prop}'.`);

  // But we can't re-assign it.
  obj.prop = "hello";
}
```

The same error appears in the docs when you write to a `readonly` tuple element (`pair[0] = "hello!"`) and when you assign to a property of a `Readonly<Todo>` object.

`readonly` doesn't make a value totally immutable. It only means the property itself can't be re-written to. To fix the error, remove `readonly`, or assign the value only in the constructor or at initialization.

