The docs I found don't define TS7006 by name. They do show it in examples where `noImplicitAny` is on and a parameter has no type annotation.

- When no type annotations are present, TypeScript falls back to `any` for a variable if it can't infer the type.
- With `noImplicitAny` on, TypeScript issues an error whenever it would have inferred `any`.
- The `noImplicitAny` example below is tagged `@errors: 7006`:

```ts
// @errors: 7006
function fn(s) {
  console.log(s.subtr(3));
}
```

So TS7006 appears to mean "parameter implicitly has an `any` type". The fix is to add an explicit type annotation, such as `s: string`.

The `implements` example in the classes page is also tagged 7006. `implements` clauses don't change how the class body is checked or its type inferred, so the parameter `s` still needs its own annotation.

